b-3ea343eb84f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054442 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the set $S.$ Solving the given inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\le 2n+1 & \\le 7 \\\\ -4\\le 2n & \\le 6 \\\\ -2\\le n & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $S$ contains all integers between $-2$ and $3$ (including $-2$ and $3$):\n\n$$\nS = \\{ -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\nA set is a subset of $S$ if all its elements are elements of $S.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $\\{ -2,0,4 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $4 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\{ 0,3 \\}$ is a subset of $S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $\\{ -3,1,3 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054443 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054443 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{ a, b, c \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c50c147-4043-5aa0-b716-a9745206fdb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054443 #edb/ref 1074 "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/83/8323a4292a4f06d66d456bfce1906cbc7e9933a3fac41dd5fb643e13d25f6a6e.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054444 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054444 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\nI. $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\nII. $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n\nIII. $\\mathbb{N}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2840f6f-da8b-519d-a191-f19d37fa7957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054444 #edb/ref 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054445 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054445 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{ n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{ n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}?$\n\nI. $A\\subseteq B$\n\nII. $B\\subseteq A$\n\nIII. $B\\nsubseteq A$\n\nIV. $B\\supseteq A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8db159d-7a1b-52ab-be96-6b1fd3eabb29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054445 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10,$ that is, $n\\geq 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5,$ that is, $n\\geq 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A.$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054629 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186054975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186054976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054630 #ed