 17592186055560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054909 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054909 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054909 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054909 #edb/ref 1045 "Subsets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054909 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186055553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054909 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054909 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054909 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186054909 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8a7c3e16-7b5a-517e-b02a-29c35f81f008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186053349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055352 #edb/ref 1069 "q-498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055352 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055352 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ (the set of non-negative rational numbers)?\n\nI. $Z$\n\nII. ${Z}^{+}$ (the set of positive integers)\n\nIII. $N$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055352 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c377d903-348b-5206-96c1-78c4296236ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055352 #edb/ref 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $Z$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{+}.$ For example, $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{+}.$\n\n- ${Z}^{+}$ is  a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{+}.$ Every positive integer is a positive rational number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{+}.$ Every natural number is a positive rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055553 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055553 #edb/ref 1061 152757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055553 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a4c7c6c6-5b36-53d1-a4de-4c76a981a433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055553 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055554 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055554 #edb/ref 1061 152758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055554 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9cee0391-700f-550d-9b6e-139dae503e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055554 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055555 #edb/ref 1061 152759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b812cc2f-347b-5ed3-979d-e5bff1beb134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055556 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055556 #edb/ref 1061 152763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "547755c4-e17d-56bf-a674-91a455f0dbbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055557 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055557 #edb/ref 1061 152764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e361262-f159-54e6-a942-659d5017ac7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055558 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055558 #edb/ref 1061 152767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055558 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5386697a-6e4f-5619-83d7-53f1064c31f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055558 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055559 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055559 #edb/ref 1061 152768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055559 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "053e7255-8298-5305-9c62-d263e5b3b5ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055559 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055560 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055560 #edb/ref 1061 152771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d545e0c5-338a-5d32-8901-ef36ab148f6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055561 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055561 #edb/ref 1061 152772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055561 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b56c42d-9c31-515e-ae69-cd2b8675b68c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055561 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055596 #edb/ref 1061 164820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055596 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7280da1b-4410-550f-9a00-d46456526bfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055596 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056044 #edb/ref 1064 "Given two sets $A$ and $B,$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we say that $A$ is a **subset** of $B,$ and we write \n\n$$\nA \\subseteq B.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ {\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{3}} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1,2,3\\}, \\qquad C=\\{1,2,{\\color{red}4}\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that *every* element of $A$ is also an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}}\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}}\\in B\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is a subset of $B,$ and we write $A \\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $A$ and $B$ as shown below. Notice that $A$ is contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/91/911dbbe9a638810f3a245fdcd004e10aee4dbd49fd8a99741c1bdd5c48cc2319.png)\n\nOn the other hand, not every element of $C$ is an element of $B{:}$ \n\n$$\n1 \\in B, \\qquad 2\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{red}4}\\notin B\n$$\n\nSince $4 \\notin B,$ we have that $C$ is *not* a subset of $B,$ and we write $C \\not\\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $B$ and $C$ as follows. Notice that $C$ is *not* contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/45/454c28bacb8d5117bb11f76988b70344b4521d260adf2b690a11e7dfd2964385.png)\n\nAnother way of thinking about the statement $C \\not\\subseteq B$ is that $C$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $B.$ In this case, that element is $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056044 #edb/ref 1065 8240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2ef02f03-d232-5ab1-88fc-1f363535caa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056044 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056047 #edb/ref 1064 "We can systematically list all the subsets of a given set using a tree diagram.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find all the subsets of the set $A=\\{ 0,1 \\}.$\n\nWe list every possible combination of elements within the set using a tree diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd51f5e02c9f280b8478e11497602dd7ae886b84d1d5994d8af73dff9719d20e.png)\n\nWe start with the empty set as the root $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}}.$ \n\nNext, we consider the element $0\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset.\n\n- If we don't include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we are left with the empty set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we get the set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}.$\n\nFinally, the list of all subsets is shown in the last column:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056047 #edb/ref 1065 8244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056047 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "daebe910-6059-595b-a45a-fc890a9b1b53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056047 #edb/ref 1067 "Listing All the Subsets of a Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056055 #edb/ref 1064 "We note the following points regarding subsets:\n\n- Every set $A$ is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\nA\\subseteq A\n$$\n\nThis is because $A$ contains the same elements as $A,$ and therefore $A$ is a subset of $A.$\n\n- The empty set is a subset of *every* set. So, for all sets $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq A.\n$$\n\nTo understand this, remember that $\\emptyset \\not\\subseteq A$ means that $\\emptyset$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $A.$ However, this cannot be true since $\\emptyset$ contains no elements! Thus, we must have $\\emptyset\\subseteq A.$\n\nIt's worth pointing out that the empty set is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFinally, note that a set $A$ is a **proper subset** of a set $B$ if $A \\subseteq B$ and $A \\neq B.$ In this case, we can write \n\n$$\nA \\subset B.\n$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}, \\qquad C = \\{ 3,2,1 \\}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a proper subset of $B,$ but $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B{:}$\n\n- Since every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we have that $A\\subseteq B.$ Also, $A\\neq B.$ Therefore, $A$ is a *proper* subset of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nA\\subset B\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $C\\subseteq B$ because every element of $C$ is an element of $B.$ However, $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B$ because $C=B.$ Therefore, we write \n\n$$\nC \\not\\subset B.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056055 #edb/ref 1065 8316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056055 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1acb16ee-d59d-5050-9848-9cfdb6e2ee6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056055 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Remarks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056056 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the natural numbers, integers, rational numbers, and real and complex numbers can be arranged in the following hierarchical diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66260e4c239137cd30453d9335256fd7d84e93a197612d670c19d97e417bd2f0.png)\n\nIn other words, $\\mathbb{N}$ is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Z},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Q},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{R},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{C}.$ We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}\n$$\n\nSince none of these sets are equivalent, we can also write\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z} \\subset \\mathbb{Q} \\subset \\mathbb{R} \\subset \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nSome restricted or extended special sets are subsets of others. For example:\n\n- $\\mathbb N$ is the set of natural numbers, and $\\mathbb N_0$ is the set of natural numbers *and* zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbb N\\subset \\mathbb N_0.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Z$ is the set of integers, $\\mathbb Z^+$ is the set of positive integers (equivalent to $\\mathbb N$), and $\\mathbb Z^+_0$ is the set of positive integers and zero (equivalent to $\\mathbb N_0$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z^+\\subset  \\mathbb Z_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Q^+$ is the set of positive rational numbers, $\\mathbb R^+$ is the set of positive real numbers, and $\\mathbb R$ is the set of real numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Q^+\\subset  \\mathbb R^+ \\subset \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nHowever, note that, for example, $\\mathbb Q \\not\\subset  \\mathbb R^+,$ since $0\\in\\mathbb Q$ yet $0\\notin \\mathbb R^+.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056056 #edb/ref 1065 8317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056056 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c336106d-52a5-5d56-a98f-c64cd26eef4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056056 #edb/ref 1067 "Subsets of Special Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056073 #edb/ref 1064 "The notation \n\n$$\nA \\supseteq B\n$$\n\nreads \"$A$ is a **superset** of $B$\", and is equivalent to $B \\subseteq A.$ This is similar to how we can write either $x \\leq y$ or $y \\geq x$. The two notations mean the same thing. \n\nSimilarly, $A \\supset B$ reads \"$A$ is a **proper superset** of $B$,\" and is equivalent to $B \\subset A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056073 #edb/ref 1065 9065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056073 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d87be2be-dac5-52e2-bcbd-7c0ad0d17032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056073 #edb/ref 1067 "Superset Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056075 #edb/ref 1064 "You might wonder why we need to define a universal set. Can't we just let the universal set be the set of everything?\n\nThe answer is no. If we don't specify a well-defined universal set, we encounter a nonsensical situation called **Russell's paradox.**\n\nFirst, suppose there exists a set $\\Omega$ that contains absolutely everything, including every mathematical object, every thought, every book, every person, animal, cell, and star in the universe, past, present, or future. \n\nRussell's paradox originates from the following idea:\n\n*Define $S$ as the set that contains all sets that are not elements of themselves.* \n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\in \\Omega\\,:\\, A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nMost sets you can think of are elements of $S.$ For example, the set $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is in $S$ because $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}\\notin \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$ \n\nIt takes a bit of thought to find sets that *aren't* members of $S.$ Such sets do exist, although typical examples are somewhat abstract: \n\n- The set containing all possible sets.\n\n- The set containing all possible thoughts.\n\n- The set $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = \\Big\\{\\underbrace{\\big\\{ \\left\\{\\{\\ldots \\}\\right\\}\\big\\}}_{X}\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nHowever, what about $S$ itself? Is $S$ an element of $S?$ There are only two possibilities:\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction because we cannot have $S\\in S$ *and* $S\\notin S.$\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we must have that $S\\in S.$ But this leads to the same contradiction as before!\n\nThe only way to resolve this is to conclude that $S\\notin \\Omega,$ but this is also a contradiction since $\\Omega$ contains absolutely everything!  So, there is no consistent answer to the question of whether $S$ itself is an element of $S.$ This is Russell's paradox, and when it was discovered, it shook the foundations of mathematics to its very core.\n\nLater, it was realized that if a set is defined using a predicate, we should ensure that all sets involved are subsets of some larger set $U$ (i.e., a universal set) where we can always determine which elements are members of $U$ and which are not, at least in principle.\n\nTo see how this resolves Russell's paradox, suppose $U$ is a universal set. Let's refine our definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\subseteq U\\,:\\,A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume $S$ is a set, so $S\\subseteq U$ for some well-defined $U.$ Once again, we'll ask the question of whether $S\\in S{:}$\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction.\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\in S.$ But this is also a contradiction.\n\nThus, to resolve the paradox, we're left with no choice but to conclude that $S\\not\\subseteq U.$ This indicates that something is fundamentally wrong with our definition of $S,$ and no such set can exist because if it were a set, it would belong to a universal set." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056075 #edb/ref 1065 9388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056075 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0e2d16c6-3382-5ba2-8d4b-949482eb4f07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056075 #edb/ref 1067 "Russell's Paradox: Why We Need a Universal Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462436 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462437 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462438 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240957 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240957 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240957 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{12},{20,22},{6,9,15}}?$\n- **I.** ${-1,{12}}$\n- **II.** ${{20,22}}$\n- **III.** ${{0},{6,9,15}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4740436b-fb84-571a-aea1-e76558938555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240957 #edb/ref 1074 "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${-1,{12}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $-1\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\n- ${{20,22}}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- ${{0},{6,9,15}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element ${0}\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462451 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462452 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462453 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240973 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of $R?$\n- **I.** ${Q}^{-}$ (the set of negative rationals)\n- **II.** ${Z}^{+}$ (the set of positive integers)\n- **III.** $N$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb139d93-059f-53af-a8f9-6a747e8ff56b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240973 #edb/ref 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is  a subset of $R.$ Every negative rational number is a real number.\n\n- ${Z}^{+}$ is a subset of $R.$ Every positive integer number is a real number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "94357139-827d-5170-82dc-cc21ec831009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253117 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253117 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253117 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253117 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b3d622c7-2460-5681-b4ae-07f1501c2299" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253213 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253213 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253213 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253213 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e90fba9d-7abb-536b-8a6d-ed108fddb75c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253516 #edb/ref 1071 "Which set is a subset of $B=\\{a,b,c,f\\}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f87155c-ad21-52b4-b2e4-c530b2e9673b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253516 #edb/ref 1074 "A set is a subset of $B$ if all its elements are elements of $B.$\n\nOf the given sets, only $\\{c,f\\}$ has all of its elements in $B.$\n\nTherefore, $\\{c,f\\}$ is a subset of $B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253526 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1189" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253526 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A=\\{1,2,3,4\\}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85335d23-39b2-5af6-837a-367b385bb13d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253526 #edb/ref 1074 "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$\n\nNotice that the empty set $\\{\\}$ is a subset of any set.\n\nTherefore, $\\{\\}$ is a subset of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $A=\\{x,y,z\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dc7abe5-6826-51d9-82bd-d471faabcb9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1074 "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/1d/1d7c11586a8fff5f63d46d936fd32c7b5fbfc33a406ddf268bfdd934fe787cdf.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,\\{x\\},\\{y\\},\\{z\\},\\{x,y\\},\\{x,z\\},\\{y,z\\},\\{x,y,z\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449091 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186256520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449092 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186256520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449093 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186256520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449094 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186256520 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1212" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256520 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256520 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N∣-1\\le x+1\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}\\le -2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⊆B$\n- **II.** $A⊈B$\n- **III.** $B⊆A$\n- **IV.** $A⊇B$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186256520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc49fe00-4ce7-5ef1-a571-3c5c05755fd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256520 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+1\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-2\\le x\\le 2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={1,2}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}\\le -2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=∅.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $0\\in A$ but $0\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. The empty set is a subset of every set.\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451247 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451248 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451249 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451250 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323898 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323898 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323898 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,…}$ and $B={5n∣n\\in N,3\\le n\\le 7}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $B⊆A$\n- **II.** $A⊆B$\n- **III.** $A⊈B$\n- **IV.** $A⊇B$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323898 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac9d3f90-ae5f-56ba-9210-f0c988989a62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323898 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the set $B.$ Substituting the values $n\\in {3,4,5,6,7}$ into the formula $5n,$ we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=3 & \\,⇒\\,5(3)=15 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,5(4)=20 \\\\ n=5 & \\,⇒\\,5(5)=25 \\\\ n=6 & \\,⇒\\,5(6)=30 \\\\ n=7 & \\,⇒\\,5(7)=35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nB={15,20,25,30,35}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350710 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350710 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350710 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350710 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "03099ae6-3de2-5dfa-8e06-fab7b23bdf08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351087 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351087 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351087 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351087 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "daa3978b-121f-5e14-b3b5-4e9953584269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351156 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $P=\\{10,20\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c97b5b71-71aa-5c9f-b66b-7c0744f33544" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351156 #edb/ref 1074 "To find every subset of $P,$ we list out every combination of elements within $P.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/17/17d796ca9f78e72d15b6f29565d03a5ea42c1e2fe544acd02bc3f368993cdb9c.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set, since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,\\{10\\},\\{20\\},\\{10,20\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351157 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351157 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $Q=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd5b17e8-78b5-59d5-b563-f6d50839e764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351157 #edb/ref 1074 "To find every subset of $Q,$ we list out every combination of elements within $Q.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process: \n\n![](images/56/56d0ff0c0e539286b6c15496c7ad295008779f07f4e9132280c787b6303902c0.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,\\{\\alpha \\},\\{\\beta \\},\\{\\gamma \\},\\{\\alpha ,\\beta \\},\\{\\alpha ,\\gamma \\},\\{\\beta ,\\gamma \\},\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395707 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395707 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395707 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395707 #edb/ref 1071 "Which set is a subset of $A=\\{a,c,e\\}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395707 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e1f8f9b-8364-5550-a922-c34dacc07366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395707 #edb/ref 1074 "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$\n\nOf the given sets, only $\\{a\\}$ has all